Titre $ HEBELOMES de Vesterholt
1,2 $ Cortina present but often fugacious; caulocystidia only present at the top of the stem; cheilocystidia with a ±.cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower half (fig. 5A-B); perispore never loosening (PO)
1,3 $ Cortina absent; caulocystidia often continuing to the base of the stem; cheilocystidia often with a differeiit shape; perispore loosening or not (PO-4)
2,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-ovoid, mostly yellowish in water and KOH mounts, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (DO-1) $ Key A - sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Hebeloma, p. 28
2,0 $ Spores ovoid-amygdaliform to citriform, mostly brownish, weakly to strongly dextrinoid (D2-S), rarely almost indextrinoid (Dl). $ Key B - sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Amygdalina, p. 29
3,0 $ Universal veil forming a membranous ring on the stem; stem continuing into a strongly rooting basc; smell of marzipan, rarely weak. $ H. radicosum, (sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Myxocybe), p. 104
3,4 $ Universal veil present or absent but never forming a membranous ring on the stem; stem rooting or not; smell not of marzipan
4,0 $ Smell strong and sweet. $ Key F - sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Sacchariolentia, p. 32
4,5 $ Smell different (radish-like, cocoa-like, soap-like, tea-like, fruit-likc) or indistinct.
5,0 $ Gills with a distinct pinkish tinge, spore deposit brownish red. $ H. sarcophyllum, (scct. Porphyrospora), p. 132
5,6 $ Gills without a pinkish tinge, spore deposit umber, sepia, dark brick or whitish
6,0 $ Fruitbodies often ± rooting; smell never with radish-like components; gills without droplets; cystidia ± cylindrical or variable in shape. $ Key E - sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Scabrispora, p. 32
6,7 $ Fruitbodies never rooting; sniell usually with radish-like components; gills with or without droplets; cystidia well differentiated and cylindrical, clavate, capitate or ventricose
7,0 $ Spore deposit whitish. $ H. domardianum (sect. Denudata, subsect. Hebelomina), p. 102
7,8 $ Spore deposit umber to sepia or dark brick
8,9 $ Cheilocystidia in majority ventricose with a cylindrical, apical part (fig. SC)
8,10 $ Cheilocystidia cylindrica1 to clavate or capitate, sometimes both widened at apex and at base (figs Sn-H)
9,0 $ Stem coarsely floccose in transverse belts, especially in the upper part, not discolouring from the base; nearly al1 cheilocystidia ventricose. $ H. sinapizans (sect. Sinapizantia), p. 60
9,0 $ Stem finely pruinose, especially in the upper part, discolouring from the base; ventricose cheilocystidia are intermixed with clavate cheilocystidia. $ H. quercetomm (sect. Velutipes, subsect. Aestiuale), p. 72
10,0 $ Spores indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (DO-2). $ Key D - sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata, p. 30.
10,11 $ Spores distinctly to strongly dextrinoid (D3-4)
11,0 $ Spores on average 4-4-5.3 µm broad. $ H. theobrominum (sect. Theobromina), p. 62
11,12 $ Spores on average > 5.5 µm broad
12,0 $ Cheilocystidia typically widened both at apex and base (fig. 5 H). $ Key D - sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata, p. 30
12,13 $ Cheilocysitida cylindrical to subclavate, typically not much widened at apex or at base (fig. 5 E)
13,0 $ Perispore never or only slightly loosening (PO-1); stem with a rounded basal bulb. $ Key C - sect. Velutipes, subsect. Velutipes, p. 30
13,0 $ Perispore distinctly loosening in most spores (P3); stem usually without a basal bulb. $ H. aestivale (sect. Velutipes, subsect. Aestiuale), p. 68